<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Widget colors come from the device's Material You palette, which exists as
  framework resources from API 31. Referencing them directly means the widget
  follows the wallpaper without the app shipping a palette of its own, and without
  any runtime code to resolve them.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Text on the widget's own panel. Paired with the panel by the -night variant. -->
    <color name="widget_background">@android:color/system_neutral1_50</color>
    <color name="widget_text_primary">@android:color/system_accent1_600</color>
    <color name="widget_text_secondary">@android:color/system_neutral2_700</color>

    <!--
      What each named text colour means, on any backdrop. Deliberately NOT duplicated in
      values-night: a tone the user named is the same tone in every configuration, and
      the whole point of naming one is that nothing moves it. Auto is the case that
      moves, and it is expressed by the qualified pairs below rather than by these.
      Tinted rather than plain white or black so the widget matches the Material You
      palette the way the platform clock widget does.
    -->
    <color name="widget_tone_light_primary">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_light_secondary">@android:color/system_neutral1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_dark_primary">@android:color/system_accent1_700</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_dark_secondary">@android:color/system_neutral1_800</color>

    <!--
      The plain pair, and the reason there is one. Everything above is tinted from the
      wallpaper, which is what makes a widget look like it belongs there and is also how
      it fails: a wallpaper with a strong accent tints the text towards itself, and text
      a few steps from what is behind it cannot be read at any size. These give that up
      and cannot fail that way. The secondary of each is the same colour softened rather
      than a second hue, because there is no palette here to take one from.
    -->
    <color name="widget_tone_white_primary">#FFFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_white_secondary">#E6FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_black_primary">#FF000000</color>
    <color name="widget_tone_black_secondary">#CC000000</color>

    <!--
      Auto on a scrim: the tone follows the system theme, because a scrim is a surface
      this app draws and it follows the theme too. Qualified rather than resolved in
      code, so a phone switched to dark mode redraws to match instead of keeping the
      tone it was built with until the next midnight. The values are the light and dark
      pairs above, split by the qualifier that decides between them.
    -->
    <color name="widget_on_scrim_primary">@color/widget_tone_dark_primary</color>
    <color name="widget_on_scrim_secondary">@color/widget_tone_dark_secondary</color>

    <!-- Behind text on the wallpaper, where the photo underneath is unknowable. -->
    <color name="widget_text_shadow">#99000000</color>

    <!--
      The two hues a scrim can be, each named for its own tone: the dark one goes under
      light text and the light one under dark text, which is the whole of what a scrim is
      for.

      Opaque, and only these two. How much of either one is actually drawn is the timer's
      ScrimStrength, applied as a tint over the shape — see WidgetPalette.scrimTintFor.
      An alpha written in here would be a second answer to the question the timer already
      answers, and the one that lost would lose silently.

      There is no Auto entry beside them, and there cannot be one: which hue Auto takes is
      decided per widget alongside the strength and handed to the launcher as a day/night
      pair, so a resource resolved under one qualifier could only ever be half of it.
    -->
    <color name="widget_scrim_dark">#FF000000</color>
    <color name="widget_scrim_light">#FFFFFFFF</color>

    <!--
      The panel at a named tone. widget_background above is the Auto one and keeps its
      -night pair; these two are its halves, pinned, for a user who named a text colour
      and must get a panel that colour can be read on whatever the phone is doing.
    -->
    <color name="widget_panel_light">@android:color/system_neutral1_50</color>
    <color name="widget_panel_dark">@android:color/system_neutral1_800</color>

    <!--
      Stands in for "some wallpaper" behind the editor's preview. A mid tone, so a
      light choice and a dark choice are both legible enough to judge against it.
    -->
    <color name="preview_wallpaper_stand_in">@android:color/system_neutral1_500</color>

    <color name="ic_launcher_background">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
</resources>
